(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-09"] = {"9.1":{"s":"A coordination compound has a central metal atom or ion bonded to surrounding molecules or ions called ligands. Each ligand gives a lone pair to the metal. Examples are the anticancer drug cisplatin, haemoglobin (iron) and chlorophyll (magnesium)."},"9.2":{"s":"Ligands are classified by how many donor atoms bind to the metal. A monodentate ligand binds through one atom (NH₃, Cl⁻, H₂O); a bidentate one through two (ethylenediamine, oxalate); EDTA⁴⁻ through six (hexadentate). A ligand that grips the metal at several points forms a ring called a chelate. An ambidentate ligand (NO₂⁻, SCN⁻) can bind through either of two atoms.","w":["EDTA⁴⁻ is hexadentate (2 N + 4 O); ethylenediamine (en) is bidentate and neutral."]},"9.3":{"s":"Key terms. The coordination sphere is the metal and its ligands, written in square brackets. Coordination number is the number of donor atoms bonded to the metal. The charge on the complex is the metal's oxidation state plus the ligand charges. Ions outside the brackets are counter ions. Shapes: 4 gives tetrahedral or square planar, 6 gives octahedral.","w":["Coordination number counts donor ATOMS, not ligands: [Co(en)₃]³⁺ has CN 6."]},"9.4":{"s":"Complexes can be grouped by their ligands: homoleptic (one kind of ligand, [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺) or heteroleptic (more than one kind). They can also be grouped by the charge on the complex ion: cationic, anionic or neutral."},"9.5":{"s":"IUPAC naming rules: name the cation first, then the anion. Inside the complex, list ligands alphabetically before the metal, using di-, tri-, tetra- for counts. Anionic ligands end in -o (chloro, cyano; newer IUPAC: chlorido, cyanido). Give the metal's oxidation state in Roman numerals. If the complex is an anion, the metal name ends in -ate (ferrate, cuprate).","w":["Ligands go in alphabetical order ignoring di-, tri-; anionic complexes end in -ate (Fe → ferrate, Cu → cuprate)."]},"9.6":{"s":"Sidgwick's effective atomic number (EAN) rule: add the metal's electrons and the electrons given by the ligands. The total often equals the atomic number of the next noble gas. This seemed to explain stability. EAN = Z − oxidation state + 2 × coordination number. Many stable complexes do not obey it, so it is only a rough guide.","f":[{"x":"EAN = Z - X + Y","n":"Z = atomic number of the metal, X = electrons lost to form the ion (oxidation state), Y = electrons donated by ligands (2 per donor atom).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 9.6, p.197"}],"w":["[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺: 27 − 3 + 12 = 36 (krypton). [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ has EAN 35 and still is stable: the rule is only a guide."]},"9.7":{"s":"Isomers have the same formula but different arrangements. Structural isomers differ in which atoms are bonded: ionisation, linkage, coordination and solvate (hydrate) isomers. Stereoisomers have the same bonds but a different arrangement in space: geometrical (cis and trans) and optical (non-superimposable mirror images).","w":["Square planar MA₂B₂ shows cis-trans isomerism; tetrahedral complexes do not."]},"9.8":{"s":"A complex's stability is measured by its stability (formation) constant: the larger it is, the more stable the complex. Stability rises with higher charge and smaller size of the metal ion, with strongly basic ligands, and with chelating ligands (the chelate effect)."},"9.9":{"s":"Valence bond theory: the metal's empty orbitals hybridise (sp³, dsp², d²sp³, sp³d²) and accept ligand lone pairs. This explains shape and magnetic behaviour. Crystal field theory: ligands split the five d orbitals into two energy sets. Strong-field ligands cause large splitting and give low-spin complexes. The splitting also explains the colours of complexes.","w":["Strong-field ligands (CN⁻, CO) pair up electrons → low spin, often inner-orbital d²sp³; weak-field (F⁻, H₂O) → high spin, outer-orbital sp³d².","The colour seen is the complement of the light absorbed in the d-d transition."]}};
